Requirements
============

This file states the hardware/software needed for each reproduction tier
described in README.md ("Reproducing the paper") and INSTALL. Tiers are
cumulative in setup but independent to run.

Software (all tiers)
---------------------
- Python 3.10, 3.11, or 3.12 (pyproject.toml: requires-python >=3.10)
- `uv` (https://github.com/astral-sh/uv) -- this project uses uv exclusively,
  never bare pip, for both the installable package and the experiments/
  reproduction pipeline.
- Git, for cloning this repository.
- No Docker/VM is required; a plain Python virtualenv (managed by uv) is
  sufficient for every tier below.

Tier T0 -- install & sanity check
----------------------------------
- Hardware: any machine, CPU only. No GPU needed.
- Software: `uv sync` (installs the core package: torch>=2.0, numpy>=1.23,
  optionally scikit-learn>=1.1 via the `metrics` extra).
- Disk: <1 GB (PyTorch CPU wheel + the package itself).
- Time: ~2 minutes.
- Network: only to fetch Python packages from PyPI.

Tier T1 -- core recompute (the Reproducible-badge claim)
-----------------------------------------------------------
- Hardware: **one CUDA GPU is required.** This is a normal requirement for an
  ML systems artifact, not the "reviewer cannot reasonably access this
  hardware" case the CODES artifact-evaluation call reserves for exotic/
  embedded hardware (e.g. specific microcontrollers) -- any current consumer
  or cloud CUDA GPU (a free Colab GPU, a university cluster node, a rented
  cloud instance) satisfies this.
- VRAM: the default per-model batch sizes in `experiments/config.py`
  (`MODEL_ATTACK_BATCH`, `MODEL_FEATURE_BATCH`) were tuned for large-VRAM GPUs
  (the paper's own hardware). The largest of the 6 core models
  (convnextv2_base) needs roughly 45 GB VRAM at its default attack batch
  (192). **On a smaller GPU, pass `--batch N` to `03_gen_adversarial.py`** to
  scale the batch down (e.g. `--batch 16` fits comfortably on an 8-16 GB
  card) -- runtime scales up proportionally, correctness is unaffected.
- Software: `uv sync --group experiments` (adds torchattacks, timm,
  pytorch-ood, h5py, pandas, fvcore, huggingface-hub, etc. -- see
  `[dependency-groups.experiments]` in pyproject.toml for the full pinned
  list).
- Disk:
    - ~250 MB for the 6 core-model checkpoints (fetched from
      `huggingface.co/amanyagami/viyog-weights` by `01_download.py
      --core-only`).
    - CIFAR-100 + the OOD benchmark sets (CIFAR-10, SVHN, STL-10, DTD, MNIST,
      FashionMNIST, Flowers102, Food101, EuroSAT, GTSRB) auto-download via
      torchvision on first use -- budget several GB free disk; sizes vary per
      dataset (a few MB to ~5 GB for the larger ones).
    - Generated adversarial examples + extracted first-conv features: a few
      GB, written under `data/adversarial/` and `results/features/`
      (both gitignored -- regenerated, never shipped).
- Network: downloads the above datasets from their standard public mirrors
  (torchvision's own download URLs) and the checkpoints from HuggingFace.
  Dataset download speed is the dominant, most variable cost of this tier --
  it is bounded by your network to those mirrors, not by this repository.
- Time: highly network-dependent (see above); GPU compute itself is fast.
  Measured end-to-end on the reference hardware (one H200, with the 6
  checkpoints already fetched): adversarial generation for all 6 core
  models x 4 attacks = 22 seconds; first-conv feature extraction across the
  ID + 10 OOD + 4 ADV splits = ~44 minutes, almost entirely the one-time OOD
  dataset downloads rather than compute (they're cached by torchvision
  afterward, so a second run is much faster); signature recomputation = 93
  seconds; the final comparison table = 1 second. Total ~46 minutes, most
  of it a one-time download, not GPU work.

Tier T2 -- extended (optional, not required for the claimed badges)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
- Same software/hardware as T1. Longer runtime and more disk (all 20
  architectures, cifar10 in addition to cifar100) because it covers more
  models/datasets, not because it needs different hardware.
- `01_download.py` (no `--core-only`) fetches the full 34-file checkpoint
  panel (~1.1 GB) from the same HuggingFace repo.

Tier T3 -- from scratch (documented, out of scope for this submission)
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
- Re-finetuning all 20 backbones from raw ImageNet-pretrained weights via
  `experiments/00_finetune.py` is GPU-weeks of compute across the full
  20-architecture x several-datasets matrix run at development time. The
  commands are in the script and its docstring for anyone who wants to
  attempt it, but it is not part of what this artifact asks a reviewer to
  do.

Known environment caveats
---------------------------
- DeepFool and CW (`--attacks deepfool cw`) are optimization-based attacks
  that are algorithmically expensive regardless of implementation library;
  the paper itself caps their evaluation-set size for this reason. They are
  excluded from the T1/T2 commands documented in README/INSTALL by default;
  add them explicitly for a fuller (slower) run.
- GTSRB's upstream mirror (`sid.erda.dk`) has had transient outages in the
  past; if `experiments/06b_extract_full.py` fails specifically on the
  gtsrb OOD split, retry later or drop gtsrb from `--attacks`/the OOD set --
  it does not block the required T1 tier, which uses cifar100 as the ID
  dataset.
